      "created_at": "2026-06-17T06:30:53Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-06-17T06:45:46Z",
      "body_html": "<p dir=\"auto\">And it turns out to be a big change, because <code class=\"notranslate\">CursorOwner::inv</code> is actually defined with the x86-specific constants. We have something like</p>\n<div class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"&amp;&amp;&amp; self.level &lt;= 4 ==&gt; {...}`\n&amp;&amp;&amp; self.level &lt;= 3 ==&gt; {....}\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><code class=\"notranslate\">&amp;&amp;&amp; self.level &lt;= 4 ==&gt; {...}`\n&amp;&amp;&amp; self.level &lt;= 3 ==&gt; {....}\n</code></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">This is wrong because there are 3-level page tables in other architectures.</p>",
      "body_text": "And it turns out to be a big change, because CursorOwner::inv is actually defined with the x86-specific constants. We have something like\n&&& self.level <= 4 ==> {...}`\n&&& self.level <= 3 ==> {....}\n\nThis is wrong because there are 3-level page tables in other architectures.",
      "body": "And it turns out to be a big change, because `CursorOwner::inv` is actually defined with the x86-specific constants. We have something like \r\n```\r\n&&& self.level <= 4 ==> {...}`\r\n&&& self.level <= 3 ==> {....}\r\n```\r\nThis is wrong because there are 3-level page tables in other architectures.",
